WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.630
In the supplied north-looking perspective, blocky hills have bedding dip slopes

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:09.950
facing toward the center. The cross section shows a down-curving fold,

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:12.270
and near-vertical cleavage cuts bedding.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:16.190
The observations support an upright or steeply inclined syncline.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.650
The eastern coarse black-and-white banded sample is gneiss;

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.850
the dull fine-grained sample with cleavage is slate.

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:28.230
Interlocking quartz grains support quartzite, while fine calcite and deformed fossils

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:32.830
support marble. Quartzite commonly began as sandstone and marble as limestone.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:37.570
A straight stream follows an intensely fractured and shattered zone between

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:40.770
the eastern banded rocks and lower-grade rocks to the west.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:43.170
The observations suggest a fault zone;

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.570
the checked Connect summary calls the boundary a reverse fault.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.370
Identify that key as supplied assessment evidence.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:53.750
The source perspective looks north, and the fold and cleavage indicate

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.390
east–west shortening in the supplied checked summary.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:59.850
The eastern banded rocks are higher grade than the rocks in

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:01.650
the grassy area and southern hill.

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:05.690
Separate those source-specific relations from rules for every syncline.
